MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m12",
  "summary": "Understand how vehicle modules communicate over networks such as CAN, read different signal types, use a scan tool for diagnosis, and find network and driver-assistance faults safely.",
  "outcomes": ["Explain why vehicles use networks and name CAN, LIN and FlexRay and what a gateway does.","Describe how the CAN bus works, including twisted pair wiring, termination and the differential signal.","Identify analogue, digital, PWM and frequency signals and measure them with a meter or scope.","Use a scan tool for codes, live data, freeze frame, actuator tests and guided diagnostics, respecting security and licences.","Test network wiring and diagnose communication faults, module power and earth faults and parasitic drain.","Describe driver-assistance sensors, tyre pressure monitoring and immobiliser systems, and the need for calibration."],
  "cheatsheet": ["A vehicle network lets modules share data over a few wires instead of many separate ones.","CAN uses two twisted wires, CAN High and CAN Low, with a 120 Ω terminating resistor at each end.","With the battery disconnected, resistance between CAN High and CAN Low on a good network is about 60 Ω.","CAN idles at about 2.5 V on both wires. A message drives High up to about 3.5 V and Low down to about 1.5 V.","A gateway connects networks that run at different speeds or use different protocols.","A U code shows a communication fault, often caused by power, earth or wiring problems at a module.","Low battery voltage can cause many false communication codes. Test the battery first.","Calibration of cameras and radar is needed after windscreen, bumper or alignment work.","Never alter safety or security systems without authorisation and the correct procedure."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m12-l1", "title": "Why vehicles use networks", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A modern vehicle can have dozens of computers, called **modules**, controlling the engine, transmission, brakes, airbags, doors, climate control and displays. They need to share information. This lesson explains how."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The problem with separate wires"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If every module needed a separate wire for each signal, the harness would be huge, heavy, costly and unreliable. For example, the vehicle speed is needed by the engine, the transmission, the instrument cluster, the brakes and the radio. Instead of five wires from the sensor, one module reads the sensor and sends the value onto a shared network."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Multiplexing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Multiplexing** means sending many messages on one pair of wires, one after the other, each with a label that tells the receiving modules what it contains. Each module listens and uses only the messages it needs. This reduces the wiring and lets makers add new features through software."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common protocols"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Protocol","Typical use","Features"],"rows":[["CAN (Controller Area Network)","Engine, transmission, brakes, body","Two-wire twisted pair, typically 500 kbit/s for high-speed networks"],["LIN (Local Interconnect Network)","Simple devices such as mirrors, seats, rain sensors","Single wire, slow and cheap"],["FlexRay","Safety-critical and chassis systems on some vehicles","Very fast and predictable timing"],["MOST or Ethernet","Infotainment, cameras","Fast, for large amounts of data"]],"caption":"Vehicle network types"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Gateways and topology"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **gateway** is a module that connects networks, passing messages between them and translating if needed. For example, a high-speed CAN network for the engine may be joined to a slower CAN network for the doors and a LIN network for the mirrors. The gateway also protects the vehicle's critical networks from the diagnostic connector. A network may be laid out as a **bus** (modules along one line), a **star** (all modules connected to a central point) or a combination."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How much wire does a network save?"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Without a network, a modern vehicle would need several kilometres of wire. A network replaces many single-purpose wires with a pair. This saves weight and cost, and a lighter loom reduces fuel use. It also means the system is more reliable because there are fewer connections to corrode. The price is that the vehicle now depends on a working network, and a fault on a bus can affect many functions at once."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Protocol","Typical speed","Wires","Typical use"],"rows":[["LIN","Up to 20 kbit/s","One signal wire","Mirrors, seats, rain sensor, small motors"],["Low-speed CAN","Up to 125 kbit/s","Two wires","Comfort and body systems"],["High-speed CAN","500 kbit/s to 1 Mbit/s","Two wires","Engine, gearbox, brakes"],["CAN FD","Up to 5 Mbit/s in the data phase","Two wires","Newer powertrain and safety"],["FlexRay","10 Mbit/s","Two or four wires","Chassis and safety-critical"],["Automotive Ethernet","100 Mbit/s or more","Two wires (a twisted pair)","Cameras, radar, infotainment, programming"]],"caption":"Speeds in kilobits or megabits per second. Higher speeds carry more data."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Power modes and sleep"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A module is supplied in several ways. **Terminal 30** is a permanent battery feed. **Terminal 15** is switched by the ignition. **Terminal 31** is earth. When the vehicle is locked, modules go to **sleep** to save power: each stops sending, and the network goes quiet. A message, a door opening or a remote signal **wakes** the network. A module that keeps waking the others, because of a fault, a stuck switch or water in a connector, keeps the whole vehicle awake and drains the battery. A vehicle can have more than 50 modules, so the sleep current of each one matters."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Because modules share a supply, a bad earth or a poor connection at the battery can cause many different symptoms. Always check the main power and earth connections early in any network fault."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A single faulty module can disturb a whole network. If one module shorts a wire or sends rubbish, others may stop communicating."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Find the network layout diagram for the vehicle at the start of any network fault. It shows which modules are on which bus and where the gateway is."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m12-l2", "title": "The CAN bus", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The Controller Area Network, or CAN, is the most common vehicle network. This lesson explains how it works and what you should see when you measure it."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"can-bus"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wiring and termination"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A high-speed CAN network uses two wires twisted together, called **CAN High** (CAN-H) and **CAN Low** (CAN-L). Twisting reduces electrical interference. A **terminating resistor** of 120 Ω is fitted at each end of the bus, often inside the two modules at the ends. The two resistors are in parallel, so the total resistance measured between CAN-H and CAN-L, with the battery disconnected and the system off, should be about 60 Ω."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The differential signal"},
      {"t":"p","text":"CAN works by the **difference** in voltage between the two wires. When no message is being sent (the recessive state), both wires rest at about 2.5 V. To send a message, the transmitter pulls CAN-H up to about 3.5 V and CAN-L down to about 1.5 V (the dominant state). The receiver reads the difference of about 2 V. Because noise affects both wires the same way, the difference is not changed, and the signal is clear."},
      {"t":"table","head":["State","CAN-H","CAN-L","Difference"],"rows":[["Recessive (idle)","About 2.5 V","About 2.5 V","About 0 V"],["Dominant (active)","About 3.5 V","About 1.5 V","About 2 V"]],"caption":"Typical high-speed CAN voltages"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Speed and messages"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Typical speeds are 500 kbit/s for powertrain and chassis, and lower speeds for body networks. Each message has an **identifier** that shows what it is and its priority, followed by the data. All modules receive every message and use only what they need. If two modules transmit at once, the one with the higher priority message wins, and the other waits."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What a scope shows"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An oscilloscope shows CAN-H and CAN-L as mirror images of each other, switching between 2.5 V and 3.5 V (or 1.5 V) in rapid bursts. A clean, symmetrical pattern shows a healthy network. A flat line at one voltage, a missing wire, or a distorted shape points to a wiring fault or a module that is pulling the bus."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading a message"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Data bytes carry numbers in coded form. To read a value, the receiver is told which bytes to use, the scale and the units. An engine speed message may use two bytes, with a scale of 0.25 rpm for each count."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Decoding engine speed","steps":["A message contains the two data bytes 0x1F and 0x40. Together they make the number 0x1F40.","In decimal, 0x1F40 = 1 × 4,096 + 15 × 256 + 4 × 16 + 0 = 4,096 + 3,840 + 64 = 8,000.","With a scale of 0.25 rpm per count, the engine speed is 8,000 × 0.25 = 2,000 rpm.","You do not decode these by hand with a scan tool, which does it for you. Knowing how it works helps you understand what a scan tool shows, and why a wrong scale gives a wrong reading."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Setting up a scope on CAN"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Connect channel A to CAN-H and channel B to CAN-L, both referenced to a good earth.","Set both channels to 1 V per division, with a DC coupling and the zero position of each at a different line so the traces do not overlap.","Set the time base to 10 or 20 microseconds per division at 500 kbit/s, which shows several bits.","Trigger on a falling edge of CAN-L, a little below 2 V.","With the ignition on, look for the idle level of 2.5 V and the bursts of activity. Compare the high and low levels with the expected values."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"If the resistance between CAN-H and CAN-L reads about 120 Ω, one terminating resistor is missing or a wire is open. If it reads close to 0 Ω, the wires are shorted together."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Make resistance and voltage measurements at the diagnostic connector first. This is the easiest place to reach and often shows if the main bus is healthy."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m12-l3", "title": "CAN in depth: frames, arbitration, load and fault patterns", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The CAN bus is simple in principle, but its details explain how a fault shows itself. In this lesson you will see what is in a message, how modules take turns, how a bus becomes overloaded and what a scope shows when a wire is shorted or open. These facts turn a vague \"communication fault\" into a specific test."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What a message contains"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Field","Size","What it does"],"rows":[["Start of frame","1 bit","Marks the start of a message and synchronises the modules"],["Identifier","11 bits (standard) or 29 bits (extended)","Says what the message is, and gives its priority. A lower number has a higher priority."],["Control","6 bits","Includes the number of data bytes"],["Data","0 to 8 bytes (up to 64 on CAN FD)","The information, such as engine speed or a switch state"],["Checksum (CRC)","16 bits","Lets receivers check that the message is correct"],["Acknowledge","2 bits","Any receiver that got the message correctly pulls the bus to the dominant state"],["End of frame","7 bits","Marks the end of the message"]],"caption":"A CAN frame with eight data bytes is about 111 bits long including the gap between messages."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Arbitration: who speaks first"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Any module may start sending when the bus is idle. If two start together, they compare the identifier bit by bit as they send. A dominant bit (0) overrides a recessive bit (1). A module that sends a 1 but sees a 0 on the bus knows that a higher-priority message is also being sent, stops and waits. The winner carries on, without any loss of its message. This means the most important messages, such as brake and engine data, have the lowest identifiers and are never delayed."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Arbitration in action","steps":["Module A sends identifier 0x100 (binary 001 0000 0000). Module B sends 0x180 (binary 001 1000 0000).","The first three bits are the same. At the fourth bit, A sends 0 and B sends 1. The bus is dominant (0), so B sees a mismatch and stops.","A's message, with the lower identifier, goes through and B tries again when the bus is idle."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Speed, timing and bus load"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Bit time = 1 ÷ bit rate","caption":"At 500 kbit/s, one bit lasts 1 ÷ 500,000 = 2 microseconds"},
      {"t":"example","title":"How busy is the bus?","steps":["A message of 111 bits at 500 kbit/s takes 111 × 2 µs = 222 µs.","The bus can therefore carry at most 1 ÷ 0.000222 = about 4,500 messages each second.","Suppose 20 different messages are each sent every 10 ms, which is 100 times a second. That is 2,000 messages a second, and the bus load is 2,000 × 222 µs = 0.44, or 44%.","Designers keep the load well under about 50% so urgent messages are not delayed. A faulty module that sends too much, or keeps re-sending because of errors, can overload the bus."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Error handling"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Every module checks every message. A module that finds an error sends an error flag and the message is sent again. Each module keeps counters of errors it sees and sends. If its own count becomes too high, the module goes **error-passive** and then **bus-off**, disconnecting itself from the network so it cannot disturb the others. This is why a faulty module may disappear from the bus, and why a scan tool reports it as not responding. Cycling the power may bring it back for a while."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Low-speed (fault-tolerant) CAN"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Comfort networks often use low-speed CAN at about 125 kbit/s. Each wire has its own driver, and the receivers can still work with one wire shorted or broken, at a reduced quality. The idle levels are different from high-speed CAN, so use the manual's voltages. A vehicle with a body network fault may keep working, but with warning messages or intermittent faults."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What a scope shows when something is wrong"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","What the scope shows"],"rows":[["Healthy bus","CAN-H between about 2.5 and 3.5 V and CAN-L between about 2.5 and 1.5 V, mirror images"],["CAN-H shorted to earth","CAN-H stuck near 0 V. CAN-L moves normally, or is distorted."],["CAN-L shorted to battery voltage","CAN-L stuck high near battery voltage"],["CAN-H and CAN-L shorted together","Both lines flat at about 2.5 V, with no activity"],["CAN-H open","Only CAN-L moves, and CAN-H is flat, at least on one side of the break"],["Missing termination","Rounded, ringing or distorted square edges with large overshoots"],["Electrical noise or a poor connection","Spikes, hash or dropouts on one or both lines"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Switch inputs and Hall sensors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A simple switch input has a **pull-up** or **pull-down** resistor inside the module. With the switch open, the resistor holds the input at a known level, high or low. Closing the switch changes the level. A dirty or worn switch can make the signal chatter briefly, called **contact bounce**, and the module filters it. A **Hall-effect sensor** has a small chip that sees a magnet's field change and switches a transistor, giving a clean square wave. It needs a supply voltage, an earth and a signal wire, and its output is often an open-collector type that needs a pull-up resistor in the module. An **inductive sensor** has no supply and generates its own signal, so the wave grows with speed."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"CAN faults leave patterns. Use a scope to see them, and use a meter on resistance and voltage to confirm what the scope shows."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Check the bus at the diagnostic connector first, pins 6 and 14. If the bus looks healthy there, the fault is in a branch or a module."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m12-l4", "title": "LIN, FlexRay, Ethernet and the wiring of networks", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"CAN is only one of several networks on a modern vehicle. Others are slower, cheaper or faster, and each has its own faults and rules. This lesson introduces them and describes how to look after the wiring of all networks."},
      {"t":"h","text":"LIN bus"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**LIN** (Local Interconnect Network) uses a single wire at up to 20 kbit/s, with one **master** module that controls the timing and up to 15 **slave** devices. The wire idles at battery voltage and is pulled down to send a message. LIN is used for simple devices such as a window switch, a rain sensor, a seat motor or a mirror, to avoid the cost of a full CAN node. A scope shows a square wave between close to battery voltage and close to earth. A fault in a slave usually affects only its own function, but a short on the wire can disable all the devices on that line."},
      {"t":"h","text":"FlexRay"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**FlexRay** is a high-speed network of 10 Mbit/s with two channels, designed for safety systems that need data to arrive at an exact time, such as active suspension and steering. It does not use arbitration. Each module has a time slot. It is more costly, and more demanding to work on, so a fault is rarely repaired by anything but the manual's test and replacement of parts."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Automotive Ethernet and MOST"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Ethernet** has appeared on vehicles for cameras, radar and software updates, where a lot of data must be carried. A single twisted pair carries 100 Mbit/s or more. **MOST** (Media Oriented Systems Transport) is a fibre-optic ring used on some cars for audio and video. Fibre cables must never be kinked or bent sharply, because light cannot go round the bend and the ring is broken. Never look into the end of a fibre cable."},
      {"t":"h","text":"K-line and older systems"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Before CAN, diagnostic communication used the **K-line** on pin 7 of the diagnostic connector, at about 10 kbit/s, and SAE J1850 on pins 2 and 10 on some makes. You may still meet them on older vehicles. A scan tool must support the protocol of the vehicle. If the tool cannot connect to an older car, check that the right protocol is selected."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Gateways"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **gateway** links the networks and keeps them apart. It passes only the messages that are needed. It also controls access from the diagnostic socket, and on some newer vehicles a secure gateway blocks the scan tool until the technician logs in with the maker's credentials. This protects the vehicle against hacking but means a workshop needs the right tools and subscriptions to work on many new vehicles."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Care of network wiring"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["CAN and FlexRay cables are **twisted pairs**. Keep the twist when you repair them, and keep the wires together and of the same length.","Repair only as the maker allows. Some manuals require a complete section of cable. A poor repair adds resistance and noise.","Never run a network cable beside high-current wires or ignition leads. Keep the original route and clips.","Use sealed connectors, because moisture in a data wire can cause intermittent faults that are very hard to find.","Shielded cables must have their shields connected to earth only where the maker says."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Network","Wires and voltage","Typical fault sign"],"rows":[["High-speed CAN","Two twisted wires, about 2.5 V at rest","Many modules offline, U codes, no communication"],["Low-speed CAN","Two wires, different levels","Comfort functions fail, intermittent"],["LIN","One wire, near battery voltage at rest","One switch, mirror or sensor stops working"],["FlexRay","Two or four wires, 10 Mbit/s","Chassis or safety warning lights, need a specialist"],["Ethernet","One twisted pair, 100 Mbit/s","Camera or display faults, update failures"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Types of scan tool"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","What it can do","Limits"],"rows":[["Basic OBD reader","Reads and clears generic engine codes","Does not read most other systems. Little live data."],["Enhanced generic tool","Reads engine and some other systems on many makes","Limited coding and special tests"],["Professional multi-make tool","Full system scans, live data, actuator tests, coding, resets, and many procedures","Needs subscription updates, and costs more"],["Maker-specific tool","Complete access for that brand, including programming","Works with only one maker, requires licences"],["PC-based interface and software","Flexible and can be updated. Often used with the maker's software.","Needs a laptop and good power supply"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"The best tool for a workshop depends on the vehicles it serves. Look at coverage, update cost, support and whether it offers the service functions your customers need, such as brake service mode, steering angle reset and ADAS calibration. Keep the software up to date, because new models and new functions are added all the time."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Wires that carry data are sensitive. Treat them with care, keep the twist and the route, and repair them as the maker allows."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never look into a broken fibre-optic cable or connector, and never bend one tightly. The light can harm the eye and the fibre will be damaged."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m12-l5", "title": "Signal types and sensors", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Modules receive information from sensors as different kinds of electrical signals. Knowing the type of signal tells you how to measure it and what a normal signal looks like."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"signal-types"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Analogue signals"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **analogue** signal varies smoothly in voltage. A throttle position sensor, for instance, gives a voltage that rises smoothly from about 0.5 V to 4.5 V as the throttle opens. A meter shows the value. Abrupt jumps or dropouts show a worn sensor track."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Digital signals"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **digital** signal switches between two states, high and low, or on and off. A switch input, such as a brake switch or a door switch, is digital. The meter will read about 0 V or about 5 V or battery voltage."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pulse-width modulated (PWM) signals"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **PWM** signal switches on and off at a fixed frequency, with the **duty cycle**, which is the percentage of time it is on, carrying the information. A 25% duty cycle is on for a quarter of the time. PWM is used to control actuators such as fuel pump speed, fan speed and boost control valves. A meter that reads duty cycle shows the percentage. A meter that reads DC volts shows the average voltage."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Frequency signals"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a **frequency** signal, the information is in the number of pulses per second. Wheel speed sensors and Hall-effect crankshaft sensors give a frequency that rises with speed. A meter with a hertz range, or a scope, shows the frequency."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reference voltage and signal return"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many sensors use three wires: a **reference voltage** (usually 5 V from the ECU), a **signal** wire and a **signal return** (ground). Two-wire sensors may be a variable resistor or voltage generator. If the reference is missing, the sensor can not work. Checking reference, signal and return in that order finds most sensor wiring faults."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Signal","Example","How to read it"],"rows":[["Analogue","Throttle position sensor","DC volts"],["Digital","Brake switch","DC volts: high or low"],["PWM","Fan speed control","Duty cycle % or scope"],["Frequency","Wheel speed sensor","Hertz or scope"]],"caption":"Reading signals"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Frequency from a wheel speed sensor","steps":["A tyre has a rolling circumference of 1.985 m. At 100 km/h the vehicle covers 100,000 ÷ 3,600 = 27.8 m each second.","Wheel speed = 27.8 ÷ 1.985 = 14.0 revolutions per second.","The sensor ring has 48 teeth, so the frequency is 14.0 × 48 = 672 Hz.","At 10 km/h the frequency is about 67 Hz, so the ABS module must be able to read a wide range of frequency, from a few hertz to over a kilohertz."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Reading a PWM signal on a meter","steps":["A fan control signal has a supply of 12 V and a duty cycle of 25%.","A meter on the DC volts range shows the average: 12 × 0.25 = 3 V. At 75% duty it shows 9 V.","A scope shows the actual waveform, and a meter set to duty cycle shows 25% or 75%. A meter that reads 12 V or 0 V at all times suggests the signal is not switching."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Finding an intermittent network fault"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Ask when it happens: when cold or hot, over bumps, in rain, after a particular action.","Run the scan and look at history codes. A code with a count of occurrences and a timestamp helps to see a pattern.","With the scan tool or scope running, wiggle the harness and connectors one section at a time, and watch for the fault to appear or the pattern to break up.","Look for corrosion and moisture at connectors under the seats, in the door hinge, near the battery and at the boot.","Check the earth points. A loose earth that makes contact only sometimes causes random errors.","Use data logging to record the fault while you drive, and review it afterwards."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If you can see the signal on a scope, you can see its shape, noise and dropouts, and it often tells you more than a meter reading."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Know what normal looks like before you test. Compare the signal with the manual's values or a known good vehicle."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m12-l6", "title": "Scan-tool diagnosis", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A scan tool is the starting point of almost all modern diagnosis. This lesson covers its functions and how to use it well."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading codes and data"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Connect the tool to the diagnostic connector, usually under the dashboard. Select the vehicle, and run a **full system scan** to read codes from every module, not just the engine. Record the codes, their status (current, pending or history) and the **freeze-frame data**. Use **live data** to watch sensor values change as you operate the vehicle."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Actuator tests and guided diagnostics"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many tools have **actuator tests** (bidirectional controls) that make a module switch a component on and off, such as a fan, a fuel pump or an injector, so you can test it without removing anything. Some tools also give **guided diagnostics**, step-by-step test plans linked to a code. They save time, but they do not replace understanding."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Network communication faults"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Codes that start with U show that modules are not talking to each other, for example a lost communication with the ABS module. The code does not say which part has failed. It shows which module is not being heard. The cause may be the module itself, its power or earth, the wiring or the network."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Coding and programming"},
      {"t":"p","text":"After replacing some modules, the new one must be set up. **Coding** sets options for the vehicle. **Programming (flashing)** loads software into the module. These procedures need the right tool, a stable battery supply and, often, an online account with the maker. A failed flash can ruin a module, so keep the battery connected to a charger that supplies steady voltage."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Security and licences"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Immobiliser and security functions are protected so that thieves can not misuse them. You must follow the maker's procedures and only work on a vehicle with the owner's authority. Do not use tools to bypass security systems. Software and information are licensed, and you must use them only as the licence allows."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Making the most of live data"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Select only the data you need.** The more items you ask for, the slower the update. Select a few related items so the display refreshes quickly enough to catch changes.","**Graph the values.** A graph shows trends and relationships. Compare short-term fuel trim with the oxygen sensor, or desired and actual rail pressure.","**Record a road test.** Many tools record data, so you can review it afterwards. Mark the moment the fault happens.","**Compare with a known good vehicle** or the manual's typical values.","**Save the report.** A report with the codes, the data and the date is useful for the customer and for warranty claims."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How an ultrasonic parking sensor measures distance"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An ultrasonic sensor sends a short burst of sound, above the range of hearing, and listens for the echo from an obstacle. Sound travels at about 343 metres per second in air at 20 °C. The module times the echo and halves the result, since the sound has gone out and back."},
      {"t":"example","title":"How far is the obstacle?","steps":["The echo returns 5.8 milliseconds after the pulse was sent.","Total distance travelled = 343 × 0.0058 = 1.99 m.","The obstacle is half that distance away, 1.99 ÷ 2 = 0.99 m, or about one metre.","Snow, mud, ice or a sticker on the sensor, or a wrongly painted bumper, changes the pattern and can cause false alarms or no alarm. Keep the sensors clean and fit the right type after a repair."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The scan tool shows what the module thinks, not necessarily the truth. Check sensor values against a measurement before you act on them."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Always save or print a full system scan before and after a repair. It proves the repair worked, and no new faults were created."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m12-l7", "title": "Diagnostic protocols, coding and software updates", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A scan tool does more than show a code. It holds a conversation with each module, asking questions and receiving answers in a defined language. Understanding that conversation explains why some tools can do more than others, why some functions need a login and what can go wrong with coding and updates. This lesson covers the main points."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Request and response"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The tool sends a request to a module, and the module sends a response. A request may ask for the stored fault codes, the value of a sensor, the software version or to run a test. The language is described by standards such as **KWP2000** (ISO 14230) and **UDS** (Unified Diagnostic Services, ISO 14229). The messages travel on the vehicle's CAN network, or over Ethernet on newer vehicles. Each module has its own address, and the tool talks to one at a time."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Service","What it does"],"rows":[["Read fault codes","Lists the codes stored, with status and extended data such as how many times the fault has occurred"],["Read data by identifier","Reads live values such as engine speed or a software number"],["Clear codes","Erases stored codes and resets the monitors"],["Input/output control","Runs actuator tests, such as switching on a fan"],["Routine control","Runs a procedure such as a relearn or a forced regeneration"],["Write data (coding)","Changes settings in the module"],["Download and transfer","Loads new software into the module (flashing)"],["Security access","A challenge and response that unlocks protected functions"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fault code formats"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Besides the five-character OBD-II code, many modules use a longer code with a status byte. The status says whether the fault is **active** (present now), **intermittent**, **stored** (seen before but not now), or **pending** (seen once, awaiting confirmation). A fault that was stored a month ago is probably not your fault, while an active one is. Read the status as well as the code."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Coding, adaptation and programming"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Task","What it is","Example"],"rows":[["Coding","Setting options in a module to match the vehicle","Telling a new body module the car has electric mirrors and a sunroof"],["Adaptation","Teaching a module a learned value","Learning the throttle closed position, or a new injector's correction"],["Programming (flashing)","Loading new software into the module","Updating the engine software to fix a fault"],["Key learning","Pairing keys to the immobiliser","Adding a spare key"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Doing it safely"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Connect a battery support unit (a stabilised power supply), not just a charger, so the voltage stays steady at about 13 to 14 V during the job.","Switch off all the loads, close the doors and make sure that the vehicle will not go to sleep.","Use the maker's software and a good quality interface. Do not use cheap or pirated tools for programming.","Follow the steps in order and do not interrupt. Do not turn the ignition off, open a door or disconnect the cable during a flash.","After the work, clear the codes, run a full scan and test the system."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Remote and over-the-air updates"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Newer vehicles can be updated over the air, through the vehicle's own mobile connection. The customer may be asked to approve an update, and the vehicle must have enough battery charge. Technicians should be aware that an update may change behaviour or features, and should check service information for updates before diagnosing a fault, since the fix may be a software update."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Data, privacy and law"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A scan tool can read personal information, such as location history or paired phones. Treat it with respect, and do not copy or share it. Odometer and security data must never be altered illegally. Software and service information are licensed. Use them only for the purpose and the vehicles the licence allows."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Never start a coding or flashing job without a steady power supply and the right procedure. A failed flash can leave a module dead."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Write down the original coding before you change it. If something goes wrong, you can restore it."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m12-l8", "title": "Network faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Network faults can seem confusing because many warning lamps come on at once. A methodical approach separates them into a few causes."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Start with the basics"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the battery voltage. A weak battery can cause many modules to lose communication or reset, and set many U codes. Charge it or connect a support unit before testing.","Look for recent work, damage or water in the vehicle.","Run a full scan and note which modules are not responding and which are.","Use the network diagram to find what the missing modules have in common."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Module power, earth and wiring"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A module that does not respond may not have power or earth. Check the fuses, and use the wiring diagram to measure voltage at the module's power and ignition pins, and test the earth with a voltage-drop test. A good module with a poor earth can behave very strangely."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["With the battery disconnected, measure the resistance between CAN-H and CAN-L. About 60 Ω is normal.","About 120 Ω suggests one end of the bus is open. Near 0 Ω shows a short between the wires.","Measure from each wire to earth and to battery positive with the power off. There should be no connection.","With the ignition on, measure each wire's voltage. CAN-H should be about 2.6 V and CAN-L about 2.4 V at rest."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Isolating a module"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A failed module can pull a bus down. Unplug modules one at a time and watch whether the bus resistance or the scan returns to normal. Disconnect the vehicle's power first, and follow the manual."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Water, connectors and parasitic drain"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Water in a connector, especially under the carpet or in a door, causes corrosion and intermittent faults. Inspect connectors for moisture and green or white powder, clean them with contact cleaner and dry them."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Modules should go to sleep a short time after the vehicle is locked. A module that stays awake drains the battery. Use the parasitic drain test (module 10) and remove fuses until the drain falls, and then look at the circuit's modules."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Example faults and tests"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Measurement (ignition off, battery disconnected)","Result","Meaning"],"rows":[["Resistance across CAN-H and CAN-L","About 60 Ω","Both terminating resistors present. Normal."],["Resistance across CAN-H and CAN-L","About 120 Ω","One resistor missing or an open wire in the bus"],["Resistance across CAN-H and CAN-L","Near 0 Ω","The wires are shorted together"],["Resistance across CAN-H and CAN-L","OL (open)","Both resistors missing, or the bus open at the diagnostic connector"],["CAN-H to earth, or CAN-L to earth","Low resistance","A wire shorted to earth"]]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Many U codes after a jump start","steps":["A car shows ten warning lights and many U codes after a flat battery was jump started.","The battery is charged and the vehicle scanned again. The codes are recorded as history, not current. Clearing them, no codes return, and the lights go out.","The cause was low voltage during starting, which made modules reset and lose contact for a moment. There was no real network fault. Always check battery voltage, and clear the codes, before you start to replace modules."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never use a test lamp on network wires. Never cut into the CAN wires to take a reading. Back-probe the connector or use the diagnostic connector."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Most U codes are caused by power, earth, wiring or battery problems, not a failed module. Eliminate them before you replace a computer."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m12-l9", "title": "Driver assistance and body electronics", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Many vehicles now have systems that help the driver or warn of danger. This lesson covers the main ones and the care needed when working on them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Sensors for driver assistance"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Ultrasonic sensors:** in the bumpers, for parking aids. They send sound pulses and time the echo.","**Cameras:** behind the windscreen or in the bumpers, for lane keeping, sign reading and parking views.","**Radar:** in the front bumper or grille, for adaptive cruise control and automatic emergency braking."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Calibration"},
      {"t":"p","text":"These sensors must point in exactly the right direction. After work that moves them, such as replacing a windscreen, a bumper, a radar bracket, or after a wheel alignment or suspension repair, the system must be **calibrated**. Calibration uses targets and a scan tool, in a level, well-lit area, and following the maker's procedure. A system that is out of calibration can brake when it should not, or fail to brake when it should."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Key and immobiliser systems"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The key contains a transponder chip that sends a code to the vehicle. The immobiliser module checks it, and allows the engine to start only if it matches. Keyless systems use a radio link. Lost keys need to be programmed by an authorised person with the right equipment. Do not attempt to bypass these systems."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Tyre pressure monitoring"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Direct TPMS** has a sensor with a battery inside each wheel that sends the pressure by radio. The sensors need to be registered to the vehicle after replacement or tyre rotation on some models. **Indirect TPMS** uses the ABS wheel speed sensors to detect a tyre that rolls with a smaller diameter, and needs a reset after inflation. The warning lamp comes on when pressure is low."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Tyre pressure monitoring in detail"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Item","Direct TPMS","Indirect TPMS"],"rows":[["Sensor","A pressure sensor and battery in each wheel","Uses the ABS wheel speed sensors"],["What it measures","Actual pressure, and often temperature","A drop in rolling diameter as a tyre loses pressure"],["Battery","Lasts five to ten years, then the sensor must be replaced","None"],["After a tyre change","Handle sensors with care. The system may need a relearn.","Reset after setting the pressure"],["Common faults","Dead sensor battery, damaged valve, corrosion, wrong sensor","A reset not done, mixed tyre sizes"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"When tyres are fitted, take care not to damage the sensor with the tyre changer. Fit the correct valve and torque the sensor nut to the specification. Keep a record of the sensor identification numbers if the system needs them."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Driver-assistance and safety systems protect lives. After any repair near them, check for warning lamps, run a scan and perform the required calibration. Never leave a system disabled."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Check that you have the right procedure for the windscreen type. Many windscreens carry the camera mount, and a wrong glass can cause calibration to fail."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m12-l10", "title": "Driver-assistance sensors and calibration", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Driver-assistance systems, often called ADAS, now fit even to modest vehicles. They use cameras, radar and ultrasonic sensors to warn the driver or to act for them. They only work if each sensor is pointing the right way. Windscreen replacement, bumper repair, suspension work and wheel alignment are now calibration jobs as well. This lesson explains the sensors, what each is used for and how calibration is done."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The sensors"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Sensor","How it works","Typical range","Used for"],"rows":[["Ultrasonic","Sends a sound pulse and times the echo","About 0.2 to 2.5 m","Parking aid, blind-spot detection at low speed"],["Short-range radar (24 GHz)","Sends radio waves and times the reflection","Up to about 30 m","Blind-spot, rear cross-traffic"],["Long-range radar (77 GHz)","As above, narrower beam, longer reach","Up to about 200 m","Adaptive cruise control, automatic emergency braking"],["Camera (mono or stereo)","A forward-looking image sensor and software","Tens of metres","Lane keeping, sign reading, pedestrian detection, auto high-beam"],["Surround cameras","Cameras on each side, joined into one view","A few metres","Parking view"],["Lidar (some vehicles)","Laser pulses timed to build a 3D map","Up to about 100 m","Advanced driving aids"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why calibration matters"},
      {"t":"example","title":"A small error becomes a big one","steps":["A forward radar is aimed one degree off. At a distance of 100 m, a one-degree error moves the aiming point sideways by 100 × tan(1°) = about 1.75 m.","That is half a lane. The radar may look at the wrong lane and brake for a car that is not in the vehicle's path, or fail to see a car that is.","This is why even small movements of a bracket or a windscreen need the system to be calibrated."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"When calibration is needed"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["After replacing or removing a **windscreen** that holds a camera or rain sensor.","After replacing or repairing a **bumper**, grille or **radar** bracket, or when the sensor has been moved.","After a **wheel alignment** that changes the thrust angle, or after a suspension or steering repair, which changes the direction the vehicle points.","After changing the **ride height** or the **tyre size**.","After replacing a module, or after a collision, even a minor one.","When the scan tool or the vehicle shows a fault or a message about the system being unavailable."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Types of calibration"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it is done","Needs"],"rows":[["Static","The vehicle is placed in front of targets or panels at set distances, and the scan tool teaches the sensor","A level, well-lit area of the right size, correct target boards, and a measuring frame"],["Dynamic","The vehicle is driven on a suitable road at a set speed, with clear lane markings, for a distance until the system learns","Good weather, good road markings and a compatible scan tool"],["Combined","A static step followed by a road drive","Both"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Preparing for calibration"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check for codes and repair any other faults first.","Set the tyre pressures and the fuel level as the maker requires, and remove heavy loads. Make sure the vehicle is on a flat floor.","Make sure that the wheel alignment is correct, because it is the reference for the sensors.","Clean the windscreen and the sensors. Check the camera and radar for damage and the mounts for play.","Place the targets exactly as the procedure says. Use a tape or a laser to measure the distances.","Run the calibration with the scan tool, and print the result.","Road test and check the system works. Record the work on the job card."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Working responsibly"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Never switch off or disable a driver-assistance system unless the customer or a procedure requires it, and never leave it disabled. If you cannot calibrate a system, say so, give the customer a note, and send them to someone who can. Never allow the vehicle to leave with a warning light for a system you have worked on. Tell the customer what the systems can and cannot do: they assist the driver, and do not drive the vehicle."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A sensor that is not aimed correctly does worse than doing nothing, because the driver trusts it. Calibrate after any work that could move it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Radar and lidar have safe power levels, but never stare into a laser or into an open sensor of unknown type. Follow the manual."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m12-l11", "title": "Connected vehicles and cyber security", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Vehicles are now connected to phones, to the internet and to the maker. This brings convenience and new services, and new risks. A technician now needs to understand telematics, updates and the basics of cyber security, so that work on the vehicle does not harm the systems or the customer's privacy."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What is connected"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Telematics unit:** a modem with a SIM card that sends data to the maker or a service provider, and supports emergency calls, remote locking, tracking and updates.","**Bluetooth and Wi-Fi:** link phones for calls, music and sometimes a phone as a key.","**Infotainment system:** a computer that runs apps, navigation and sometimes the vehicle settings.","**Diagnostic and charging connections:** the diagnostic socket, and the charging port on electric vehicles, can carry data.","**Aftermarket devices:** trackers, insurance monitors and dongles plugged into the diagnostic socket can talk to the vehicle network."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why security matters"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Everything that can send a message to the vehicle network is a possible way in. Researchers have shown that a weakly protected connection can allow someone to affect the locks, the lights or even the brakes. Makers now fit **gateways**, **secure boot** (the module only runs trusted software), signed software updates and encrypted keys. An aftermarket device plugged into the diagnostic socket can undo some of this protection if it is poorly made."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Threat","What it could do","What a technician can do"],"rows":[["Relay attack on a keyless entry","A thief boosts the key signal to unlock and start the car","Advise the customer to keep the key in a signal-blocking pouch, and check the key's settings"],["Rogue diagnostic dongle","Opens a way to the vehicle network","Tell customers not to leave unknown dongles in the socket"],["Unauthorised software","Changes the vehicle's behaviour","Use only the maker's or licensed software, and keep it updated"],["Public charging or Wi-Fi","A route for malware or data theft","Update the vehicle software and be careful with unknown networks"],["Stolen personal data","Phone contacts and locations on the infotainment","Delete a customer's data from a vehicle before it is sold or serviced if asked"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Updates"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Software updates fix faults and close security holes. They are delivered by a technician with a tool, or over the air. Always check the maker's information for updates when a fault appears, and install them as the procedure says. Do not interrupt an update, and do not install unofficial software."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Handling customer data"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["When you scan a vehicle, you may see the VIN, mileage, location and phone details. Treat these as private.","Do not post or share data, screenshots or reports without permission.","If a customer sells the vehicle, advise them to remove their accounts and phone pairings, and to reset the infotainment system.","Keep the workshop's computers and diagnostic equipment secure, with passwords and updates."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Remote services and emergency calls"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many vehicles have a **remote services** app that can show the vehicle's location, lock or unlock the doors, start the climate control and report faults. In Europe and some other regions, new vehicles must have **eCall**, which dials the emergency services automatically after a serious crash and sends the position. These functions need the telematics unit to have power, a SIM card and a signal. A weak 12 V battery can reduce the range of the unit, and a faulty unit can drain the battery when the vehicle is parked."},
      {"t":"p","text":"If a customer reports a flat battery and the vehicle has a connected unit, include it in the parasitic drain test. Check that the unit goes to sleep, and be aware that some units wake from time to time to send data, so the measured current may rise briefly."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The diagnostic socket is a door into the vehicle. Treat it with care, and do not leave unknown devices connected."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If a customer asks about tracking, explain what data the vehicle sends, and how they can switch off services in the settings."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Measure CAN bus signals and read module data","objective":"Measure the resistance and voltages on a CAN network, interpret them, and use a scan tool to read module codes and live data.","time":"75 minutes","tools":["Digital multimeter with fine probes or breakout leads","Oscilloscope if available","Scan tool that supports all modules","Wiring diagram and network layout for the training vehicle","Notepad and a results table"],"safety":["Disconnect the battery before you measure bus resistance.","Do not cut or pierce any network wire. Use the diagnostic connector or back-probe.","Keep hands and leads away from the running engine's belts and fans.","Do not clear codes or run actuator tests without the instructor's approval.","Do not code or program any module on the training vehicle."],"steps":["Find the network layout diagram and identify the CAN bus pins on the diagnostic connector.","With the battery disconnected, measure the resistance between CAN-H and CAN-L. Record the value.","Reconnect the battery and turn the ignition on. Measure the voltage on CAN-H and CAN-L to earth.","If a scope is available, display both wires and sketch the pattern.","Connect the scan tool and run a full system scan. List every module that responds.","Record all codes with their status and note any U codes.","Open live data for the engine module and watch coolant temperature and engine speed.","Open live data for the ABS or body module and compare wheel speeds or door status as you operate them.","Compare your readings with the expected values and mark each as normal or not normal.","Write down which findings suggest a wiring, power, or module fault, if any.","Switch off, remove the tools and restore everything to its original condition."],"checks":["I measured bus resistance with the power off and understood the result.","I measured bus voltages and compared them with the expected values.","I ran a full system scan and recorded modules and codes.","I did not damage any wiring or change any module settings.","I explained what each measurement shows."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m12-q01","q":"Why do vehicles use a network instead of separate wires for every signal?","options":["It removes the need for sensors on the engine and the brakes","It makes every module run at the same speed all the time","It reduces wiring and lets modules share information","It stops any single module from ever failing in service"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l1","explain":"A shared bus carries many messages on two wires and cuts weight, cost and faults."},
    {"id":"m12-q02","q":"What does a gateway do?","options":["It stores the fault codes for the entire vehicle for the owner","It supplies the power for all of the modules on the vehicle","It connects networks and passes messages between them","It amplifies the signal from the battery to the starter motor"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l1","explain":"A gateway links networks of different speed or type and protects critical ones."},
    {"id":"m12-q03","q":"Which network type is a single-wire, slow and cheap system used for mirrors and seats?","options":["LIN","Ethernet","CAN High","FlexRay"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l1","explain":"LIN suits simple devices that need little data."},
    {"id":"m12-q04","q":"What is multiplexing?","options":["Using many separate wires for every single signal in the vehicle","Sending many messages along the same pair of wires, one after another","Running two engines from one fuel tank through a single pump","Joining two batteries in series to give a higher supply voltage"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l1","explain":"Each message carries an identifier, and modules use what they need."},
    {"id":"m12-q05","q":"Which network protocol is typically used for simple devices like mirrors and seats?","options":["FlexRay","LIN","MOST","Ethernet"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l1","explain":"LIN uses a single wire at up to 20 kbit/s and is cheap, so it suits small devices. FlexRay and Ethernet are faster and more costly."},
    {"id":"m12-q06","q":"How many wires does a high-speed CAN bus use?","options":["Two twisted wires, called CAN High and CAN Low","Four wires, one for each module on the bus","Three wires: power, earth and signal","One wire that carries the data and the earth together"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l2","explain":"The twist reduces interference, and the signal is the difference between the wires."},
    {"id":"m12-q07","q":"What is the resistance between CAN-H and CAN-L on a healthy network with the battery disconnected?","options":["About 0 Ω","About 120 Ω","About 12 kΩ","About 60 Ω"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l2","explain":"Two 120 Ω resistors in parallel give about 60 Ω."},
    {"id":"m12-q08","q":"A reading of about 120 Ω between CAN-H and CAN-L means what?","options":["The battery is flat and the modules have all gone to sleep at once","The network is perfectly healthy and both of the terminating resistors are present","The two wires are shorted together somewhere along the length of the bus","One of the terminating resistors is missing or the bus is open"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l2","explain":"Only one 120 Ω resistor is being measured, so there is a break between the two ends."},
    {"id":"m12-q09","q":"In the recessive (idle) state, what voltage is on each CAN wire?","options":["About 0 V on both wires","About 5 V on both wires","About 2.5 V on both wires","About 12 V on CAN-H and 0 V on CAN-L"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l2","explain":"The difference between the wires is near zero."},
    {"id":"m12-q10","q":"What happens to the CAN-H and CAN-L voltages in the dominant state?","options":["Both wires fall to 0 V while the message is being sent","CAN-H falls to about 1.5 V and CAN-L rises to about 3.5 V","CAN-H rises to about 3.5 V and CAN-L falls to about 1.5 V","Both wires rise to about 12 V as the message is sent along the bus"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l2","explain":"The receiver reads the voltage difference of about 2 V."},
    {"id":"m12-q11","q":"Why are the CAN wires twisted?","options":["To make the two wires easier to identify by their different colours","To reduce the effect of electrical interference","To make the wires stronger and harder to break when they are pulled","To increase the voltage between the wires of the pair on the bus"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l2","explain":"Interference affects both wires equally, and the difference stays the same."},
    {"id":"m12-q12","q":"On a CAN bus, which message has the highest priority?","options":["The one with the highest identifier number","The one sent first by any module","The one with the most data bytes","The one with the lowest identifier number"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l3","explain":"A dominant bit (0) overrides a recessive bit (1), so a lower identifier wins arbitration. Critical messages have low identifiers."},
    {"id":"m12-q13","q":"A CAN frame of 111 bits is sent at 500 kbit/s. How long does it take?","options":["222 microseconds","111 microseconds","2 microseconds","500 milliseconds"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l3","explain":"Each bit lasts 2 µs, so 111 × 2 = 222 µs. This limits the bus to about 4,500 messages a second."},
    {"id":"m12-q14","q":"A bus carries 2,000 messages a second, each taking 222 µs. What is the bus load?","options":["About 4.4%","About 100%","About 44%","About 444%"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l3","explain":"Load = messages per second × time per message = 2,000 × 0.000222 = 0.444, or 44%. Designers keep it under about 50%."},
    {"id":"m12-q15","q":"What does it mean when a module goes bus-off?","options":["It disconnects itself after errors","It has been switched off by the driver","It is sending data at full speed","It has been updated with new software"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l3","explain":"Each module counts errors. If its count is too high, it removes itself from the bus so it cannot disturb the others."},
    {"id":"m12-q16","q":"CAN-H and CAN-L are shorted together. What does a scope show?","options":["CAN-H at 5 V and CAN-L at 0 V","A normal mirror-image pattern","Both lines flat at 2.5 V","Both lines at battery voltage"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l3","explain":"With the wires shorted, there is no difference voltage, so no messages can be sent. The lines sit near the idle level."},
    {"id":"m12-q17","q":"What is the termination resistance measured across CAN-H and CAN-L with the power off?","options":["About 120 Ω","About 12 Ω","About 600 Ω","About 60 Ω"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l3","explain":"Two 120 Ω resistors in parallel give 60 Ω. A reading of 120 Ω shows that one resistor is missing or the bus is open."},
    {"id":"m12-q18","q":"Why must fibre-optic network cables not be bent sharply?","options":["The cable would get too hot to touch","Light leaks out and the ring breaks","The fibre would become electrically live","The cable would change its colour"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l4","explain":"A tight bend lets the light leak out of the fibre. The signal is lost and the network stops."},
    {"id":"m12-q19","q":"What is a gateway on a vehicle network?","options":["A mechanical door in the firewall","A fuse for the data wires","A module linking networks","A sensor on the diagnostic socket"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l4","explain":"A gateway connects networks that run at different speeds or protocols, and controls what passes between them and from the socket."},
    {"id":"m12-q20","q":"A repair is made to a twisted CAN pair. What must be kept?","options":["The wires as far apart as possible","The twist and the wires together over the repair","The wires of different lengths","The twist removed for neatness"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l4","explain":"Twisting reduces noise. A repair with the wires apart or untwisted picks up interference and can cause faults."},
    {"id":"m12-q21","q":"A throttle position sensor gives a voltage that rises smoothly from 0.5 V to 4.5 V. What type of signal is this?","options":["Frequency","Analogue","Digital","PWM"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l5","explain":"An analogue signal varies smoothly over a range."},
    {"id":"m12-q22","q":"A brake pedal switch gives either about 0 V or about 12 V. What type of signal is this?","options":["Digital","Analogue","CAN","Frequency"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l5","explain":"A digital signal has only two states, such as on and off or high and low, so a meter reads roughly 0 V or a fixed higher voltage."},
    {"id":"m12-q23","q":"In a PWM signal, what carries the information?","options":["The colour of the wire and the pin that it is fitted into","The number of wires that there are in the connector for the part","The height of the voltage only, measured on a meter across the wires","The duty cycle, the percentage of time the signal is on"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l5","explain":"Fan and pump speed controls often use PWM, with a higher duty cycle for more power."},
    {"id":"m12-q24","q":"How does a wheel speed sensor usually send its information?","options":["As a frequency that rises with speed","As a steady 5 V level that does not change with the road speed","As a resistance that does not change when the wheel turns","As a pulse width that stays at a fixed 50% duty cycle"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l5","explain":"The faster the wheel turns, the more pulses per second."},
    {"id":"m12-q25","q":"What are the three wires of a typical three-wire sensor?","options":["Battery positive, battery negative and the horn circuit of the car","Fuel, spark and air for each of the cylinders in the engine","Reference voltage, signal and signal return","Brake, clutch and throttle pedal switches in the cabin footwell"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l5","explain":"Checking reference, signal and return finds most wiring faults."},
    {"id":"m12-q26","q":"A wheel speed sensor has a ring with 48 teeth. The wheel turns 14 times a second. What is the frequency?","options":["14 Hz","672 Hz","48 Hz","3.4 Hz"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l5","explain":"Frequency = revolutions per second × teeth = 14 × 48 = 672 Hz."},
    {"id":"m12-q27","q":"A PWM fan signal of 12 V has a 25% duty cycle. What does a DC voltmeter show?","options":["About 12 V","About 6 V","About 0 V","About 3 V"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l5","explain":"A meter averages the signal: 12 × 0.25 = 3 V. A scope shows the switching."},
    {"id":"m12-q28","q":"What is freeze-frame data?","options":["A list of all the codes that have ever been stored on the vehicle","A snapshot of data at the moment a fault was stored","A picture taken by a camera of the vehicle at the time of the fault","A record of the owner's driving style over the last year"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l6","explain":"It shows the conditions when the fault occurred."},
    {"id":"m12-q29","q":"What does an actuator test (bidirectional control) allow you to do?","options":["Measure the battery voltage with the ignition key switched off","Reprogram the module to a newer version of the software from the maker","Clear all the codes in every module in the vehicle at once","Command a component on and off with the scan tool"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l6","explain":"You can test a fan, pump or injector without removing it."},
    {"id":"m12-q30","q":"What does a U code show?","options":["A low tyre pressure in one of the wheels of the vehicle","A communication fault between modules","A failed spark plug in one of the cylinders of the engine","A faulty brake pad wear sensor on one of the front wheels"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l6","explain":"It shows that a module is not being heard, but not why."},
    {"id":"m12-q31","q":"Why should the battery be supported with a charger while programming a module?","options":["A charger is needed to unlock the security system on the vehicle","A weak battery will make the programming faster and more reliable","The programming tool charges the battery by itself while it is running","A voltage drop during programming can ruin the module"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l6","explain":"A failed flash can leave the module unusable."},
    {"id":"m12-q32","q":"Why are security and immobiliser functions protected?","options":["To protect the electronic parts from being cleaned or handled","To prevent theft, so they can only be changed by authorised procedures","To make repairs slower and more expensive for every workshop and owner","To stop the vehicle from being sold on to another owner later"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l6","explain":"Follow the maker's procedure and have the owner's authority."},
    {"id":"m12-q33","q":"What does a pending fault code mean?","options":["The fault has been repaired","The fault is not important to the driver","The fault was cleared by the customer","A fault seen once, not yet confirmed"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l7","explain":"A pending code shows a fault seen once. If it returns on the next drive cycle, it becomes confirmed and the lamp comes on."},
    {"id":"m12-q34","q":"What is the difference between coding and programming?","options":["Coding sets options; programming flashes","Coding loads software. Programming sets options","Coding clears codes. Programming reads codes","They are exactly the same job"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l7","explain":"Coding adjusts settings in a module so that it matches the vehicle. Programming (flashing) replaces its software."},
    {"id":"m12-q35","q":"Why use a battery support unit during flashing?","options":["The unit tells the flash tool the vehicle's VIN","The voltage must stay steady","The unit makes the flash complete faster","The unit is required to clear fault codes"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l7","explain":"If the voltage drops during a write, the module may be left with incomplete software and stop working."},
    {"id":"m12-q36","q":"What should you do before changing the coding of a module?","options":["Disconnect the diagnostic socket","Switch off the vehicle's lights","Remove the fuse of the module","Write down the original coding"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l7","explain":"The original coding lets you restore the module if the change gives problems."},
    {"id":"m12-q37","q":"What should you check first when many U codes appear?","options":["Clear the codes and ignore them, since they will not come back","Replace the gateway module and then run the scan tool again","Replace the scan tool, since it must be giving false codes","The battery voltage and the module power and earth connections"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l8","explain":"Low voltage or poor earths cause many false communication faults."},
    {"id":"m12-q38","q":"The resistance between CAN-H and CAN-L measures near 0 Ω. What is the likely fault?","options":["The battery is overcharged and the voltage is too high for the bus","The bus is working normally and no fault is present","The two wires are shorted together","A terminating resistor is missing from one end of the bus"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l8","explain":"Zero ohms shows a direct connection between the two wires."},
    {"id":"m12-q39","q":"How can a failed module that is pulling down the bus be found?","options":["Unplug modules one at a time and check whether the bus returns to normal","Run the engine at a fast idle and watch the bus on the scope","Replace all the modules on the bus at once and see if it recovers","Disconnect the alternator belt and run the engine at high speed"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l8","explain":"Isolate with the battery disconnected and follow the manual."},
    {"id":"m12-q40","q":"A car's battery goes flat overnight. A module stays awake after locking. What is the best test?","options":["A parasitic drain test, removing fuses to find the circuit","A compression test of each of the cylinders in turn on the engine","A wheel alignment check on the front and rear axles of the car","A cooling system pressure test with the engine cold and the cap on"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l8","explain":"Removing fuses one at a time shows the circuit where the current falls, and then you can look at the module that is on that circuit."},
    {"id":"m12-q41","q":"Why must you never use a test lamp on network wires?","options":["It will make the signal clearer on the scope that is attached","It can overload and damage the network and the modules","It will not light on a low voltage and will give a false reading","It will only work on the CAN-L wire and not on the CAN-H wire"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l8","explain":"A test lamp draws too much current for network wires, so use a meter, a scope or back-probing at the connector instead."},
    {"id":"m12-q42","q":"Which test measures a network's wiring health with the battery disconnected?","options":["Oil pressure at the main gallery at a fast idle speed","Fuel pressure at the fuel rail with the engine running at idle speed","Coolant temperature at idle with the thermostat fully open at last","Resistance between the two bus wires"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l8","explain":"With the power off, the bus resistance shows whether both terminating resistors are present and the wires are intact, without needing the network to be active."},
    {"id":"m12-q43","q":"Many U codes appear after a jump start and the battery is now good. What should you do first?","options":["Replace the ABS module and retest","Clear the codes and see if they return","Replace the gateway before anything else","Replace the battery again and retest"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l8","explain":"Low voltage can reset modules and set history codes with no real fault. Clear them, and check whether they return as current."},
    {"id":"m12-q44","q":"With the power off, CAN-H to CAN-L reads 120 Ω. What does this mean?","options":["The bus is normal, with both resistors","The wires are shorted together at a point","The bus has three resistors in circuit","A terminating resistor is missing"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l8","explain":"Normal is about 60 Ω. A reading of 120 Ω shows that only one resistor is in circuit."},
    {"id":"m12-q45","q":"When is calibration of a driver-assistance camera needed?","options":["After replacing a windscreen or a part that holds the camera","After washing the vehicle and polishing the bonnet and the roof","After changing the engine oil and the oil filter at a service","After rotating the tyres from the front axle to the rear axle"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l9","explain":"The camera must point in exactly the right direction."},
    {"id":"m12-q46","q":"What does indirect TPMS use to detect a low tyre?","options":["The brake fluid pressure in the lines that go to each caliper","The ABS wheel speed sensors, which show a tyre rolling with a smaller diameter","A battery-powered sensor that is fitted inside each wheel of the vehicle","The fuel pressure in the rail that supplies each of the injectors"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l9","explain":"Indirect systems compare wheel speeds, so they need a reset after the tyres are inflated or changed, and they cannot show the exact pressure."},
    {"id":"m12-q47","q":"Which system uses a transponder chip in the key to stop the engine starting without the right code?","options":["The immobiliser","The airbag","The cruise control","The ABS"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l9","explain":"It checks the code before allowing the engine to start."},
    {"id":"m12-q48","q":"A repair leaves a driver-assistance warning lamp on. What should you do?","options":["Pull the fuse and tell the customer that the system is not needed","Remove the bulb so the lamp does not show on the dashboard any more","Scan the system, fix the fault and perform any required calibration","Clear the code and release the vehicle to the owner as it is"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l9","explain":"Driver-assistance and safety systems protect lives, so a warning lamp must never be hidden or ignored; the fault must be repaired and the system calibrated."},
    {"id":"m12-q49","q":"What is a difference between direct and indirect TPMS?","options":["Direct has a sensor in each wheel","Direct is cheaper and has no battery at all","Indirect measures the tyre temperature only","They are exactly the same in every way"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l9","explain":"Direct sensors measure pressure and have a battery that wears out. Indirect systems detect a smaller rolling diameter with the ABS sensors."},
    {"id":"m12-q50","q":"Which sensor is often used for adaptive cruise control over long distances?","options":["A long-range radar","An ultrasonic sensor","A tyre pressure sensor","A coolant temperature sensor"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l10","explain":"A 77 GHz radar has a narrow beam and long range, so it can track vehicles ahead at highway distances."},
    {"id":"m12-q51","q":"A radar is misaligned by 1° and the target is 100 m ahead. About how far off is the aim point?","options":["About 0.0175 m","About 17.5 m","About 1.75 m","About 100 m"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l10","explain":"Offset = 100 × tan 1° = about 1.75 m. That is half a lane, so the radar can look at the wrong lane."},
    {"id":"m12-q52","q":"Which job needs a driver-assistance camera to be calibrated?","options":["Replacing a wiper blade on the screen","Topping up the washer fluid reservoir","Changing a rear light bulb for another","A new windscreen carrying the camera"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l10","explain":"The camera is mounted on the glass. A new windscreen puts it in a slightly different position, so its view must be calibrated."},
    {"id":"m12-q53","q":"What is a static calibration?","options":["Driving on a motorway at a set speed","Cleaning the camera lens with a cloth","Teaching the sensor with targets placed in front of the vehicle","Replacing a sensor battery"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l10","explain":"Targets at set distances give the sensor known references. Dynamic calibration is done by driving on suitable roads."},
    {"id":"m12-q54","q":"After a wheel alignment, why might driver-assistance systems need calibration?","options":["Its centreline reference has moved","The tyres have been made rounder","The wheels are now lighter","The brake fluid has changed colour"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m12-l10","explain":"The sensors reference the vehicle's centreline and thrust angle. A change in alignment can alter that."},
    {"id":"m12-q55","q":"What is a relay attack on keyless entry?","options":["A thief cuts the fuel line","A thief boosts the key signal","A thief changes the engine oil","A thief removes the wheels"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m12-l11","explain":"Two devices relay the signal between the car and a key that is far away, making the car think the key is near."},
    {"id":"m12-q56","q":"Why should an unknown dongle not be left in the diagnostic socket?","options":["It uses up the engine oil","It makes the tyres wear faster","It can open the vehicle network","It changes the speedometer reading"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l11","explain":"The socket connects to the vehicle networks. A poorly made device can let in attackers or send false messages."},
    {"id":"m12-q57","q":"Why check for software updates when a fault appears?","options":["A software fix may be available","Updates always make the vehicle slower","Updates remove the warranty","Updates clear all the faults"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m12-l11","explain":"Some faults are known software issues. A bulletin or an update may fix the cause, and save time and parts."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m12-c01","front":"What is a gateway?","back":"A module that connects networks and passes messages between them.","lesson":"m12-l1"},
    {"id":"m12-c02","front":"What is multiplexing?","back":"Sending many messages along the same wires, one after another.","lesson":"m12-l1"},
    {"id":"m12-c03","front":"What is LIN?","back":"A slow, single-wire network for simple devices such as mirrors and seats.","lesson":"m12-l1"},
    {"id":"m12-c04","front":"What is CAN?","back":"A two-wire network used for engine, transmission, brake and body modules.","lesson":"m12-l1"},
    {"id":"m12-c05","front":"Why are CAN wires twisted?","back":"To reduce the effect of interference.","lesson":"m12-l2"},
    {"id":"m12-c06","front":"What resistance should a healthy CAN bus read between H and L?","back":"About 60 Ω with the power off.","lesson":"m12-l2"},
    {"id":"m12-c07","front":"What does about 120 Ω between H and L mean?","back":"One terminating resistor is missing or the bus is open.","lesson":"m12-l2"},
    {"id":"m12-c08","front":"What are the CAN voltages when idle?","back":"About 2.5 V on both wires.","lesson":"m12-l2"},
    {"id":"m12-c09","front":"What are the CAN voltages when active?","back":"CAN-H about 3.5 V, CAN-L about 1.5 V.","lesson":"m12-l2"},
    {"id":"m12-c10","front":"Why does a lower CAN identifier win?","back":"A dominant 0 bit overrides a recessive 1 bit, so the lower number keeps the bus.","lesson":"m12-l3"},
    {"id":"m12-c11","front":"How long is one bit at 500 kbit/s?","back":"2 microseconds. A 111-bit frame takes 222 µs.","lesson":"m12-l3"},
    {"id":"m12-c12","front":"What is the bus load formula?","back":"Messages per second times the time of one message. Keep under about 50%.","lesson":"m12-l3"},
    {"id":"m12-c13","front":"What does bus-off mean?","back":"A module disconnects itself from the bus after too many errors.","lesson":"m12-l3"},
    {"id":"m12-c14","front":"CAN-H and CAN-L shorted together on a scope?","back":"Both lines flat at about 2.5 V, with no activity.","lesson":"m12-l3"},
    {"id":"m12-c15","front":"Speed and wires of LIN?","back":"Up to about 20 kbit/s on a single wire, with one master.","lesson":"m12-l4"},
    {"id":"m12-c16","front":"What is FlexRay?","back":"A 10 Mbit/s network with fixed time slots, for chassis and safety systems.","lesson":"m12-l4"},
    {"id":"m12-c17","front":"What does a vehicle gateway do?","back":"A module that links networks and controls which messages pass, including from the diagnostic socket.","lesson":"m12-l4"},
    {"id":"m12-c18","front":"What is an analogue signal?","back":"A voltage that varies smoothly, such as a throttle position sensor.","lesson":"m12-l5"},
    {"id":"m12-c19","front":"What is a digital signal?","back":"A signal with two states, on or off.","lesson":"m12-l5"},
    {"id":"m12-c20","front":"What does duty cycle mean?","back":"The percentage of time that a PWM signal is on.","lesson":"m12-l5"},
    {"id":"m12-c21","front":"How is a wheel speed signal read?","back":"As a frequency in hertz, which rises with speed.","lesson":"m12-l5"},
    {"id":"m12-c22","front":"What are the three wires of a typical sensor?","back":"Reference voltage, signal and signal return.","lesson":"m12-l5"},
    {"id":"m12-c23","front":"How do you find wheel speed frequency?","back":"Revolutions per second times the number of teeth on the ring.","lesson":"m12-l5"},
    {"id":"m12-c24","front":"What is an actuator test?","back":"A scan tool command that switches a component on and off.","lesson":"m12-l6"},
    {"id":"m12-c25","front":"What does a U code mean?","back":"A communication fault between modules.","lesson":"m12-l6"},
    {"id":"m12-c26","front":"Why support the battery when programming?","back":"A voltage drop can ruin the module.","lesson":"m12-l6"},
    {"id":"m12-c27","front":"What are UDS and KWP2000?","back":"Standard languages scan tools use to talk to modules (ISO 14229 and ISO 14230).","lesson":"m12-l7"},
    {"id":"m12-c28","front":"Coding, adaptation and programming?","back":"Coding sets options. Adaptation teaches learned values. Programming loads new software.","lesson":"m12-l7"},
    {"id":"m12-c29","front":"What must you do before flashing a module?","back":"Connect a battery support unit, switch off loads, use the maker's software and do not interrupt.","lesson":"m12-l7"},
    {"id":"m12-c30","front":"What should you check first with many U codes?","back":"Battery voltage and module power and earth.","lesson":"m12-l8"},
    {"id":"m12-c31","front":"What does near 0 Ω between CAN-H and CAN-L mean?","back":"The two wires are shorted together.","lesson":"m12-l8"},
    {"id":"m12-c32","front":"How do you find a module pulling down a bus?","back":"Unplug modules one at a time and watch the bus.","lesson":"m12-l8"},
    {"id":"m12-c33","front":"Why never use a test lamp on network wires?","back":"It can damage the network and the modules.","lesson":"m12-l8"},
    {"id":"m12-c34","front":"What is a parasitic drain caused by a module?","back":"A module that stays awake and drains the battery.","lesson":"m12-l8"},
    {"id":"m12-c35","front":"CAN resistance readings?","back":"About 60 Ω is normal. 120 Ω means one resistor missing or open. Near 0 Ω means shorted.","lesson":"m12-l8"},
    {"id":"m12-c36","front":"When is ADAS calibration needed?","back":"After windscreen, bumper or alignment work that moves sensors.","lesson":"m12-l9"},
    {"id":"m12-c37","front":"What is direct TPMS?","back":"Sensors with batteries inside the wheels that send the pressure by radio.","lesson":"m12-l9"},
    {"id":"m12-c38","front":"What is indirect TPMS?","back":"It uses ABS wheel speeds to detect a low tyre.","lesson":"m12-l9"},
    {"id":"m12-c39","front":"What does the immobiliser check?","back":"The key's transponder code, before it allows the engine to start.","lesson":"m12-l9"},
    {"id":"m12-c40","front":"Name five driver-assistance sensors.","back":"Ultrasonic, short-range radar, long-range radar, camera and lidar.","lesson":"m12-l10"},
    {"id":"m12-c41","front":"Which repairs need ADAS calibration?","back":"After a windscreen, bumper or radar repair, a wheel alignment, suspension work or a tyre or ride height change.","lesson":"m12-l10"},
    {"id":"m12-c42","front":"Static and dynamic calibration?","back":"Static uses targets in a workshop. Dynamic uses a road drive at set speeds.","lesson":"m12-l10"},
    {"id":"m12-c43","front":"What is a relay attack?","back":"Thieves extend a keyless key's signal to unlock and start a car.","lesson":"m12-l11"},
    {"id":"m12-c44","front":"Why can a diagnostic dongle be a risk?","back":"It connects to the vehicle network and can let in attackers if it is poorly made.","lesson":"m12-l11"}
  ]
});
